course: dictionary
lesson: 8544
title: Scaffolding: a frame and a helper
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «Scaffolding» is a good example. Builders use scaffolding, a frame of metal poles and wooden boards that workers stand on while they build, paint or repair. «The house was covered in scaffolding.» The clue is the house: it is being mended.
explain: In school, scaffolding means something different. It is the extra help a teacher gives you when you first learn something, such as sentence starters or a word bank. «The teacher gave us a word bank as scaffolding for our story.» The help is taken away bit by bit as you get better.
explain: The big idea behind both meanings is support. Any support that holds something up while it is being made can be called scaffolding. When you meet the word, ask: is this about a building, or about learning? The other words in the sentence will tell you.
fact: Scaffolding is a frame of metal poles and boards that workers stand on.
fact: «Never climb scaffolding.» Safety rules say children must never climb it.
fact: In school, scaffolding means extra help, such as sentence starters or word banks, which is taken away step by step.
fact: Scaffolding is uncountable, so you can say «some scaffolding», or count it as «a piece of scaffolding».
fact: Builders in Hong Kong still use «bamboo scaffolding» tied with plastic strips, though more metal scaffolding is used there too.
exercise: «The workers stood on the scaffolding to paint the top floor.» Why did they use the scaffolding? | To stop the rain | So they could sleep outside | To make the building smaller | *So they could stand high up while they painted
exercise: Mrs Okafor gives her class word banks and sentence starters to help them write. What is this kind of help called? | *Scaffolding | Foundation | Plasterboard | Guttering
exercise: Which word is a form of the verb scaffold? | *scaffolds | castles | windows | roofs
test: «Scaffolding is a temporary frame of steel poles and boards clamped together by hand by scaffolders.» What does temporary mean here? | Made of wood | Very tall | Strong enough to climb | *Only for a while, not permanent
test: Ravi's teacher takes away his sentence starters bit by bit as he improves. What does this show about the scaffolding? | Its poles are getting taller | It is breaking | *It is being taken away step by step | It is being painted
exam: Which sentence uses scaffolding in the building sense? | *The workers put up scaffolding round the church so they could repair the roof | Ella's teacher gave her a word bank as scaffolding | The scaffolding of the story is a happy ending | Scaffolding is a helpful word to know
